{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you acquire a Hebrew slave.</b> The reason that all the laws begin with the sending forth of the slave to freedom in the seventh year is that the Ten Statements begin: ״אָנֹכִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם מִבֵּית עֲבָדִים״ (Exodus 20:2). Here it says: just as you were a slave and freedom was given to you because I redeemed you from the house of slaves, so too shall you proclaim freedom for your slave who is sold to you on account of his theft — for you too were sold on account of the theft of Joseph, whom the tribes sold, and the matter rolled on so that they went down through him to Egypt, to the house of slaves, and even so you went out to freedom. So too, you shall send your slaves free." "And as for why the expression ״עִבְרִי״ is used and it does not say ״אָחִיךָ״ or ״יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — and likewise in the portion Re'eh (Deuteronomy 15:12) it says ״כִּי יִמָּכֵר לְךָ אָחִיךָ הָעִבְרִי אוֹ הָעִבְרִיָּה״ — it is because all the seed of Abraham were from the other side of the river, and there our fathers were servants of idolatry from of old. And after they entered beneath the wings of the Shekhinah they were called by the name ״יִשְׂרָאֵל.״ But this sinner who is sold on account of his theft is called by the name ״עִבְרִי,״ for he grasped the ways of his fathers of Abraham. And the expression ״עִבְרִי״ applies also to one who commits a transgression (עֲבֵרָה), and even though he sinned and committed a transgression, nevertheless he is your brother. Therefore it says ״אָחִיךָ הָעִבְרִי״ etc. But one who sells himself, who has not sinned — concerning him it says (Leviticus 25:39) ״כִּי יָמוּךְ אָחִיךָ״ without qualification, and it does not say ״הָעִבְרִי.״" "And there are those who say that it says ״עֶבֶד עִבְרִי״ to tell you that he is already a slave before you acquire him, for the deed of the Holy One, blessed be He, precedes, as it says (Leviticus 25:42) ״כִּי עֲבָדַי הֵם.״ And as for what is written ״וְאִם אָמֹר יֹאמַר הָעֶבֶד״ — because he repeated in his foolishness, it did not call him by the name עִבְרִי.\n\nAnd the reason for ״six years he shall serve״ — there are those who say that a plain שְׁנֵי שָׂכִיר is three years, as it says (Isaiah 16:14) ״וְנִקְלָה כְּבוֹד מוֹאָב בְּשָׁלֹשׁ שָׁנִים כִּשְׁנֵי שָׂכִיר,״ and a double hire was imposed upon him on account of the penalty of double restitution. And there are those who say this is not so, for behold it says elsewhere (Isaiah 21:16) ״בְּעוֹד שָׁנָה כִּשְׁנֵי שָׂכִיר,״ and its meaning is a shortened year; and thus Rashi and the Radak explained the verse ״וְנִקְלָה כְּבוֹד מוֹאָב.״\n\nAnd perhaps its reason corresponds to three thefts that he stole: the money of his owners, and the mind of his owners, and the higher Mind — corresponding to them he serves three years, and on account of the penalty of double restitution, another three years.\n\nAnd there are those who say that this is the pattern of all seventh [periods] chosen for rest, as a remembrance of the renewal of the world — like the Sabbath and the Sabbatical year and the Jubilee, for every seventh is chosen for rest. And this is more correct and near to hearing."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {2 "The word שָׁנָה in the Isaiah 21:16 citation could mean 'year' or 'repetition'; rendered literally per the context of the verse being quoted."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:15.457389154Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}